/-- Finiteness and the fibre formula at every point, including nonclosed points.

Source: BM 1997, §3 and §9; BMT §6.1 and §6.5; ILO VIII 2.3.11--2.3.13
(blueprint: lem-trf-jet-diagram-hilbert-control).

Proof plan from the owning node:
1. Choose algebra generators $a_1,\ldots,a_e$ of $A$. In $A\otimes_kA$, $J$ is generated by
$\delta_i=1\otimes a_i-a_i\otimes1$: modulo these differences the multiplication map is an
isomorphism. As a left $A$-algebra, $P^n$ is therefore a quotient of
$A[T_1,\ldots,T_e]/(T)^{n+1}$, $T_i\mapsto\delta_i$, and is spanned as an $A$-module by the
finitely many monomials of degree at most $n$. This includes $n=0$ and $A=0$.
2. Fix $x$, put $L=\kappa(x)$, $R=A_x$, and let $\mathfrak q=\ker(L\otimes_kA\to L)$ for
$\lambda\otimes a\mapsto\lambda\bar a$. Set $S=(L\otimes_kA)_{\mathfrak q}$. Tensoring the
presentation in Step 1 gives $P^n\otimes_AL=(L\otimes_kA)/\mathfrak q^{n+1}=S/(\mathfrak
qS)^{n+1}$. Indeed $\mathfrak q$ is generated by $1\otimes a_i-\bar a_i\otimes1$; modulo its
power every element outside $\mathfrak q$ is a nonzero scalar plus a nilpotent and has a finite
geometric-series inverse. Thus localization changes no such quotient. Both $R$ and $S$ have
residue field $L$. Their Noetherianity follows from finite type over a field and localization
(an ideal in a localization is generated by the images of generators of its contraction).
3. Characteristic zero makes $L/k$ separable. The smooth-subalgebra theorem writes $L$ as the
fraction field of a smooth $k$-subalgebra. Base change to $R$ and localization therefore make
$R\to S$ essentially smooth. The base-change and localization formulas for differentials
identify $\Omega_{S/R}\otimes_SL$ with $\Omega_{L/k}$; the dimension of the latter is $t_x$.
This checks the relative cotangent rank, not the dimension of the generally nonzero-
transcendence-degree local ring $R$.
4. Apply the rational-point smooth completion theorem to $R\to S$: its residue map is the
identity, its rings are Noetherian, and its relative cotangent rank is $t_x$ by Step 3. It gives
$\operatorname{gr}_{\mathfrak qS}S\simeq(\operatorname{gr}_{\mathfrak
m_x}R)[Z_1,\ldots,Z_{t_x}]$, with degree-one variables. Write $g(j)=\dim_L\mathfrak
m_x^j/\mathfrak m_x^{j+1}$. Additivity of length along the finite maximal-ideal filtration gives
$h_x(n)=\sum_{j\leq n}g(j)$: every graded piece is annihilated by $\mathfrak m_x$, so a vector-
space flag is a composition series. The same calculation in $S$, followed by grouping its
polynomial monomials by exponents, gives $\operatorname{length}_S(S/\mathfrak
q^{n+1}S)=\sum_{\beta\in\mathbf N^{t_x},\,|\beta|\leq n}h_x(n-|\beta|)=\Lambda^{t_x}h_x(n)$. Its
left side is the $L$-dimension in Step 2. Each extra variable sums the previous function from
$0$ to $n$; when $t_x=0$ the formula is $h_x$.
-/
theorem finite_and_fibre_dimension {k A : Type u} [Field k] [CharZero k]
    [CommRing A] [Algebra k A] [Algebra.FiniteType k A] :
    (∀ n : ℕ, Module.Finite A (principalParts k A n)) ∧
    ∀ (x : Spec (CommRingCat.of A)) (n : ℕ),
      fibreDimension k A (affineEvaluation x) n =
        HilbertSamuel.function
          (Spec.map (CommRingCat.ofHom (algebraMap k A))) x n := by
  sorry

/-- The centered Taylor map on the actual stalk is uniquely characterized by its residues, scalars
and coordinates.

Source: BM 1997, §3 and §9; BMT §6.1 and §6.5; ILO VIII 2.3.11--2.3.13
(blueprint: lem-trf-jet-diagram-hilbert-control).

Proof plan from the owning node:
5. On an affine etale chart $\operatorname{Spec}B\to\mathbf A^d_k$ with coordinates $u_i$, write
$X=\operatorname{Spec}(B/I)$ and $L=\kappa(x)$ for $x\in X$. Flatness and unramifiedness give
unique lifts of $B\to L$ to $L[T]/(T)^{n+1}$ over $k[u]$, with $u_i\mapsto u_i(x)+T_i$, by
successive square-zero thickenings. Set $C_n=(L\otimes_kB)/\mathfrak q^{n+1}$, where $\mathfrak
q$ is the evaluation kernel. The map $L[T]/(T)^{n+1}\to C_n$ sends $T_i$ to $1\otimes u_i-
u_i(x)$, and the lifted Taylor map gives a reverse map. Their composite on the polynomial
algebra fixes every $T_i$; the other composite fixes the left $L$ and is the identity on $B$ by
uniqueness of the lift of $B\to L$ over $k[u]$ into $C_n$. Thus they are inverse. Passing to
inverse limits defines $\tau_x:B\to L[[T]]$ and the completed-diagonal isomorphism. Quotienting
$C_n$ by the image of $I$ now gives $L[[T]]/(K_x+(T)^{n+1})=P^n_{(B/I)/k}\otimes_{B/I}L$, with
$K_x=(\tau_x(I))$. This uses the scalar-extended diagonal, without assuming a coefficient-field
section into $\widehat{\mathcal O}_{X,x}$.
-/
theorem existsUnique_taylor {k : Type u} [Field k] [CharZero k]
    {U : Scheme.{u}} {d : ℕ} (c : MvPolynomial (Fin d) k →+* Γ(U, ⊤))
    [Etale (U.toSpecΓ ≫ Spec.map (CommRingCat.ofHom c))] (x : U) :
    ∃! T : U.presheaf.stalk x →+* MvPowerSeries (Fin d) (U.residueField x),
      HilbertCoefficient.isTaylorMap
        ((U.presheaf.germ ⊤ x (by trivial)).hom.comp c) (U.residue x).hom T := by
  sorry

/-- The Taylor quotient computes the same extended local function as the jet fibre.

Source: BM 1997, §3 and §9; BMT §6.1 and §6.5; ILO VIII 2.3.11--2.3.13
(blueprint: lem-trf-jet-diagram-hilbert-control).

Proof plan from the owning node:
